for most operations you need to down_read(mm_sem) ie., a read lock

for pte updates you need PTE lock. There are two types depending upon a build configuration:
<a> single global lock per address space called page_table_lock

<b> last level page tables's per page lock  
http://lxr.linux.no/linux+v2.6.37/include/linux/mm.h#L1100
<refer #if USE_SPLIT_PTLOCKS>

so if your last level page table spans across 4 struct pages then there will be 4 separate
locks.


<The ideal dream case being CAS on the PTE ...>
The dream could be realized on architectures where the PTE spans CAS size. Trouble is unlike Solaris
HAT where locking is maintained completely within the HAT layer (and therefore by individual cpu code)
Linux does the locking in VM and then invokes the write pte routines (pte_offset_map_lock)


----------------
typical access pattern being 
pte_t* va = pte_offset_map_lock(mm, pmd, ....)
   read write modify ptes within bracket
pte_unmap_unlock(va)

The bracket above will take care of mapping/ reusing existing maps and locking and that is arch specific:

<a> x86_32 they map/unmap into kernel space each time
http://lxr.linux.no/linux+v2.6.39/arch/x86/include/asm/pgtable_32.h#L52

<b> for x86_64
all page tables are always mapped in the kernel 
Difference with seg_kpm being:
-> with the latter the entire memory is always mapped
-> I see no evidence the Linux version uses large pages so you could waste a lot of memory doing this for just holding
   your kernel page tables
http://lxr.linux.no/linux+v2.6.39/arch/x86/include/asm/pgtable_64.h#L142
